**Proposition 24.2 (task-relative resources and nontransfer).**
In these constructions the paid unit seed and restricted history
promise support the inverse in §§17 and 22; the known contractive
lattice law and a live positive report support AD's acquired bound;
the supplied $B$ supports §20's effective rational isolation.
Actual earned correspondence and current references/rights support
every selected real call. None of these numerical inverses alone
supplies an unknown law, seed, source binding, grant or physical
restoration. Finite controller descriptions do not imply bounded
lifetime storage, width or traffic.

Proof. The unit/gap hypotheses are used in (17.5) and (22.2);
without a known unit, the two alpha preparations in Boundary 4.4
already identify different initial gaps. The lattice and contraction
are separately used in (17.6) and the exact even-grid extraction;
a positive real report without the lattice would not give the latter.
Rational spacing in (20.6) uses the supplied denominator class.
Without a bound or other effective isolating promise, Theorem 19.2
excludes the unrestricted entrance-identity task. This does not make
the particular denominator format necessary for every task.

Bindings and grants are independent enabledness premises of §7;
constructing equal coordinate words cannot change a disabled call
into an enabled one. The returned-state equations (17.4), (20.5)
and (23.1) count the actual advanced clocks and contain no physical
inverse operation. Their source and report widths grow with the
actual gaps or rational inputs, and the finite loops operate on those
growing records. Thus no uniform finite backing follows from a
bounded program. These are conditional operational statements on
the declared ideal services, not empirical hardware conclusions.
$\square$

Both native generators are required for the full LAB task by the
